WN.lb(3,{"mt_1KCwbGvm1F":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which way does your finger move along a line of words?","choices":["left to right →","right to left ←","bottom to top ↑"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at the first word of the line. Which way is the next word?","explain":"In English we read each line from left to right, from the first word to the last."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is a single letter?","choices":["m","map","mum"],"answer":0,"hint":"A letter is one shape on its own. A word has letters side by side.","explain":"m is one letter. Map and mum are words, each made of three letters."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia writes {n} words in a line, with a gap between each pair. How many gaps does she leave?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Try it with two words first: how many gaps are between them?","explain":"Each gap sits between two words, so {n} words need {=n-1} gaps.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What tells you the name of a book?","choices":["the title on the front cover","the barcode on the back","the last word on the last page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the front of the book, near the top.","explain":"The title is on the front cover. It tells you what the book is called."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many words are in: I like dogs?","choices":["3","9","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tap once under each group of letters. Do not count the letters.","explain":"I, like, dogs: 3 words. There are 9 letters, but letters are not words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A page has {n} lines of words. Which line do you read first?","choices":["the top line","the bottom line","the longest line"],"answer":0,"hint":"We read down the page, one line after another. Where does down start?","explain":"We read the top line first, then each line below it in turn.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Do the words in a book say the same thing every time you read them?","choices":["yes, the words stay the same","no, they change each time","only if you look at the picture"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a story you know well. Does the grown-up read the same words each time?","explain":"Print does not change, so the words always say the same thing. The picture helps, but the words carry the story."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You read the last word on a line. What do you do next?","choices":["go back to the left and read the next line","stop, because the page is done","read the same line again backwards"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there another line below? Where does it start?","explain":"We sweep back to the left end of the next line and keep reading until the last line."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Where do you start reading a page?","choices":["the top line, on the left","the bottom line, on the right","in the middle of the page"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the very first word. Which line is it on, and which side?","explain":"English starts at the top line, at the left end, and moves along to the right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A line has {n} words. How many gaps are there between the words?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Two words have one gap between them. Count the gaps between each pair of words.","explain":"There is a gap between each pair of words, so {n} words have {=n-1} gaps. There is always one more word than gaps.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Is dog a letter or a word?","choices":["a word","a letter","a number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the letters in it. One letter on its own is a letter.","explain":"Dog has three letters side by side, d, o and g, so it is a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam reads the {n} words on the top line. He gets to the end. Where does he read next?","choices":["at the left end of the next line","straight down, at the right end of the next line","back along the top line"],"answer":0,"hint":"After each line your eyes sweep back to one side of the page. Which side?","explain":"At the end of a line we sweep back to the left and read the next line from its first word.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"The top line has {a} words. The next line has {b} words. How many words are on the page?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Point under each word on both lines and count as you go.","explain":"{a} words and {b} more words make {=a+b} words on the page.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["The words on a page tell us something. They say the same thing each time we read them.","We start at the top, on the left. Then we read along the line this way →.","At the end of a line, we jump back to the left. Then we read the next line down.","There is a gap after each word. A word is made of letters. Cat is one word. It has three letters."],"adult":["Concepts of print are the ideas a child needs before they can learn to read: print carries a fixed message, English runs left to right and top to bottom, each line ends with a sweep back to the left of the next line, and spaces mark where words begin and end.","Point under each word as you read aloud, one tap for each spoken word. This one-to-one matching shows that a spoken word matches a written word, and it helps the child see the gaps. Let the child take over the pointing on a familiar book.","Common mistakes: reading the picture instead of the words, starting on the right or in the middle, going straight down at the end of a line, and counting letters as words. Children also mix up the words letter and word, so name both: this is the letter s, this is the word sun.","Show the front cover, the title and which way the pages turn. Ask where to start and let the child point. Arabic and Hebrew run right to left, so the direction is a rule of English writing, not of all writing."]},"familyTip":"At story time, ask your child to show you the front of the book and where to start. Then guide their finger under each word as you read it aloud.","keyIdea":"Print tells us something, and it says the same thing every time. In English we read from the top line down, and along each line from left to right. At the end of a line we jump back to the left. Gaps split the words, and each word is made of letters.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Letter or word? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Letter","Word"],"items":[["s",0],["sun",1],["t",0],["top",1],["p",0],["pen",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagprint","caption":"Flagship lab: slide a reading finger along the lines, count words, sort letters from words and turn a book to its front.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_UvNrOXny1i":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many beats in elephant?","choices":["3 beats","2 beats","8 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: el, e, phant. Clap each part.","explain":"El-e-phant has 3 beats. Eight is how many letters it has, which is not the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Strong has 6 letters. How many beats does it have?","choices":["1 beat","6 beats","2 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say strong. Do you need to stop in the middle?","explain":"Strong is said in one go, so it has 1 beat, even though it has 6 letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Cake has the letters a and e. How many beats?","choices":["1 beat","2 beats","4 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cake. Is the e at the end loud or quiet?","explain":"The e at the end of cake is silent, so there is only one vowel sound and 1 beat."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What word do pop and corn make?","choices":["popcorn","cornpop","pop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say first pop, then corn, quickly together.","explain":"Pop + corn in that order makes popcorn. Gluing beats in the order you hear them builds the word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where does thunder break into beats?","choices":["thun | der","th | under","thund | er"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly and listen for where your voice stops for a moment.","explain":"You hear thun, then der, so thunder splits as thun | der, with one vowel sound in each piece."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia says ba-na-na and claps {n} times. She says banana has {n} beats. Is she right?","choices":["No, banana has 3 beats","Yes, she is right","No, banana has 6 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say ba-na-na and count the parts on your fingers.","explain":"Ba-na-na has three parts, so it has 3 beats. Six is the number of letters.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben has {n} picture cards. He wants one with 1 beat, like dog. Which card?","choices":["train","tiger","apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each word. Which one needs only one clap?","explain":"Train is said in one go, so it has 1 beat. Ti-ger and ap-ple have two beats each.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How many beats in caterpillar?","choices":["4 beats","3 beats","11 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: cat, er, pil, lar.","explain":"Cat-er-pil-lar has 4 beats. Eleven is the number of letters, not beats."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Say butterfly slowly and clap. How many beats?","choices":["3 beats","2 beats","9 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it in parts: but, then ter, then fly. Clap for each part.","explain":"But-ter-fly has three parts, so it has 3 beats (syllables). Nine is the number of letters, not beats."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kit claps {n} times for cat. Is that right?","choices":["No, cat has 1 beat","Yes, that is right","No, cat has 3 beats"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cat. Do you say it in one go, or in parts?","explain":"Cat is said in one go, so it has 1 beat. Its three letters do not make three beats.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What word do rain and bow make?","choices":["rainbow","bowrain","rain"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the parts in order: first rain, then bow, quickly.","explain":"Gluing (blending) rain + bow in order makes rainbow. The order matters: bowrain is not a word."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam claps two-beat words, like rab-bit. He claps {n} words. Which word could be next?","choices":["pen-cil","dog","but-ter-fly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Clap each word. Which one has two claps, like rab-bit?","explain":"Pen-cil has two beats, like rab-bit. Dog has one beat and but-ter-fly has three.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Where does carrot break into beats?","choices":["car | rot","ca | rrot","carr | ot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say carrot slowly. What do you hear first?","explain":"You say car, then rot, so the beats are car | rot. Each piece has one vowel sound."}],"explain":{"kid":["Words have beats. Say cat. Clap one time. Cat has one beat.","Say rab-bit. Clap two times. Rab-bit has two beats. A beat is called a syllable.","Some long words have one beat. Strong has lots of letters. Say it. Clap one time!","You can glue beats. Rain and bow make rainbow. You can cut a word too: rab and bit."],"adult":["A syllable is a beat of sound in a spoken word, built around one vowel sound. Counting, blending and splitting syllables is an early step in hearing the sound structure of words, before and alongside phonics. It helps children read longer words in chunks and spell them part by part (the English national curriculum asks Year 1 children to divide words such as rabbit, carrot, thunder and sunset into syllables).","In the lab, your child claps words such as cat, rabbit and butterfly, then compares strong with banana (six letters each, one beat against three). They glue spoken parts into rainbow, popcorn and dinosaur, chop written words at the beat breaks, and sort pictures into one, two or three beats.","Common mistakes: counting letters instead of beats, counting every vowel letter (cake has a and e but one beat, because the final e is silent), and rushing so two beats blur into one. A useful trick is to put a hand under the chin and say the word slowly: the chin drops once for each beat.","Clap names at home. Your child's name, a sibling's, the dog's: how many beats? Then say a word in beats, such as ta-ble, and let them glue it back together."]},"familyTip":"Play a clapping name game: clap each family member's name, one clap per beat (Sam, Ma-ya, Ol-i-ver). Then say a word in parts, like rain ... bow, and ask your child to say the whole word.","keyIdea":"A syllable is one beat in a spoken word. Cat has one beat, rab-bit has two and but-ter-fly has three. You can clap the beats, glue beats together to make a word, and split a written word into its beats to help you read and spell it. Count beats by listening, not by counting letters: strong has six letters but only one beat.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"One beat or two? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["1 beat","2 beats"],"items":[["cat",0],["dog",0],["train",0],["cake",0],["rabbit",1],["pencil",1],["apple",1],["tiger",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsyl","caption":"Flagship lab: clap the beats, find that letters are not beats, glue parts into words, chop written words at the beats, then sort pictures by beats.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_frDIaXzWbx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the name of the letter h?","choices":["aitch","huh","ay"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the ABC song, not the sound at the start of hat.","explain":"The name of h is aitch. huh is the sound it makes in hat, and ay is the name of a."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Turn the letter d around, like in a mirror. Which letter do you get?","choices":["b","d","p"],"answer":0,"hint":"d has its tummy first. Where is the tummy after the flip?","explain":"After the flip the stick comes first and the tummy after it, so d turns into b."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. To spell it out loud you say one letter name for each letter. How many names do you say?","answer":"n","hint":"One letter, one name. Count the letters.","explain":"{n} letters means you say {n} letter names, one for each letter.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam sings l-m-n-o-p as one word. How many letters is that really?","choices":["5","1","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each one slowly and hold up a finger for each name.","explain":"l, m, n, o and p are five letters. The song goes fast, so they can sound like one word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Is it c or k at the start of the word cat?","choices":["c, see","k, kay","Either one is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"c and k can both say k. Think of how cat is spelt: see, ay, ...","explain":"cat is spelt see, ay, tee. k can make the same sound, but cat always uses c."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Ali can say the alphabet up to letter number {n}. Z is letter number 26. How many letters are left?","answer":"26-n","hint":"Count on from {n} to 26, one letter at a time.","explain":"From letter {n} to letter 26 there are 26 take away {n}, which is {=26-n} letters left.","params":{"n":[12,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which pair is a capital and small letter of the same letter?","choices":["A and a","B and d","P and q"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the name of each shape. Do they match?","explain":"A and a are both called ay. B and d, and P and q, are different letters."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which letter comes just before S in the alphabet?","choices":["R","T","Q"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sing the ABC from P: p, q, and stop before s.","explain":"The order is q, r, s, t, so R comes just before S. T comes after it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which small letter is the partner of capital G?","choices":["g","q","c"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the name of capital G. Which small letter has the same name, gee?","explain":"G and g are one letter called gee. q has a tail too, but its name is cue."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The alphabet has 26 letters. Mia can name {n} of them. How many more letters does she need to learn?","answer":"26-n","hint":"Start at {n} and count on to 26, or take {n} away from 26.","explain":"26 take away {n} is {=26-n}, so Mia has {=26-n} more letters to learn.","params":{"n":[10,24]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which letter comes just after K in the alphabet?","choices":["L","J","M"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sing the ABC from H: h, i, j, k, ...","explain":"The order is j, k, l, m, so L comes just after K. J comes before it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Each letter has a capital and a small shape. How many shapes do {n} letters have?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each letter gives two shapes. Count two for every letter.","explain":"Two shapes for each of {n} letters makes {=2*n} shapes, but still only {n} letter names.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every letter has a name. This is b. Its name is bee. We sing the names in the ABC song. Each letter has two shapes. A big one is a capital. A small one is for most words. G and g are the same letter.","Look at b and d. Turn b around and it is d! So check which way it faces. b has the stick first. d has its tummy first.","The letters go in order, A to Z. Sing it slowly: l, m, n, o, p. That is five letters, not one long word.","A letter has a name and a sound. c is called see. In cat it says k. To spell cat, say the names: see, ay, tee."],"adult":["This is alphabet knowledge, one of the strongest early predictors of learning to read. US Common Core RF.K.1.d asks children to recognise and name all upper- and lowercase letters. The English national curriculum for Year 1 asks pupils to name the letters of the alphabet in order and to use letter names to tell apart alternative spellings of the same sound.","Keep three ideas apart. The letter name is fixed (aitch for h). The letter sound, or phoneme, is what the letter usually says in a word (h as in hat). Phonics lessons teach the sounds first for reading, so children often know sounds before names. Both are needed: names stay the same while one sound can have several spellings, so names let you talk about spelling, for example k or c.","Common mistakes: reversing b, d, p and q, because in everyday life an object turned around is still the same object (mirror generalisation); hearing l-m-n-o-p in the song as one letter, elemeno; thinking a capital and its lowercase letter are two different letters, especially pairs that look different such as A and a, G and g, R and r; and giving the sound when asked for the name.","In the lab your child drags small letters to their capital partners, finds b, d, p and q, fills gaps in an alphabet train, picks a letter's name over its sound, and spells cat, kid and dog with letter names. Be careful to say names and sounds clearly and not to mix them in one sentence."]},"familyTip":"Play I spy with letter names on signs and cereal boxes: 'I spy a capital M, em.' Then ask your child to find its small partner, m, somewhere else on the box.","keyIdea":"The alphabet has 26 letters. Each letter has a name, like bee for b, and two shapes: a capital and a small letter. Capital G and small g are one letter with one name. The letters come in a set order, from A to Z. Some letters are the same shape turned around, like b and d, so which way a letter faces matters. We use letter names to spell, for example cat is see, ay, tee.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which capital is each small letter's partner? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A","G","R"],"items":[["a",0],["ɑ",0],["g",1],["ɡ",1],["r",2],["R",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagabc","caption":"Flagship lab: match small letters to capitals, find b, d, p and q, fill the alphabet train, tell names from sounds, and spell with letter names.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_YKkCM63fSC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Can you sound out the word and?","choices":["Yes: a-n-d","No, it is a tricky word","No, you have to guess it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the sound of each letter: a, n, d.","explain":"Every letter in and says its usual sound, so it sounds out. It is common, but not tricky."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do we write the word that sounds like sed?","choices":["said","sed","sad"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a tricky word. The middle is not written the way it sounds.","explain":"We write said. The ai in the middle says e: that is its tricky part."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"You know {n} tricky words. This week you learn {k} more. How many tricky words do you know now?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Start at {n} and count on {k}.","explain":"{n} and {k} more makes {=n+k} tricky words you can read in a blink.","params":{"n":[5,12],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which part of the word you is tricky?","choices":["The ou, it says oo","The y at the start","None, it sounds out"],"answer":0,"hint":"y says y as usual. What do the next two letters say?","explain":"The ou in you says oo, not its usual sound. That is the bit to learn by heart."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You are stuck on the word they. What helps most?","choices":["Sound out th, then learn that ey says ay","Guess from the picture","Skip the word"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use what you know first, then the tricky part.","explain":"th says th as usual. The ey says ay: learn that part by heart and read they lots of times."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sentence has {n} words. You read {k} of them by sight. How many words do you still need to sound out?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the sight words away from all the words.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} sight words leaves {=n-k} words to sound out.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Was and saw use the same three letters. Are they the same word?","choices":["No, the order is different","Yes, same letters","Yes, if you read fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the first letter of each word.","explain":"The order of the letters makes the word. Was starts with w, saw starts with s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In the word of, which letter does not say its usual sound?","choices":["The f, it says v","The o","Both letters"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say of slowly. Does the end sound like f in fish?","explain":"The f in of says v, like in van. That is the tricky part of of."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sight words: which part of the word said is the tricky part?","choices":["The ai, it says e","The s at the start","The d at the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each part. Which one does not make its usual sound?","explain":"s says s and d says d, as usual. The ai says e here, so the ai is the tricky part to learn by heart."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A page has {n} words. {k} of them are the word the. How many words on the page are not the?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the number of the words away from all the words.","explain":"{n} words take away {k} is {=n-k} words that are not the. The word the is so common it is worth knowing in a blink.","params":{"n":[20,30],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The letters are w, a, s. Which word is it?","choices":["was","saw","wish"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at the first letter. Which word starts with w?","explain":"w, a, s is was. Saw has the same letters in a new order, so it starts with s."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You read the word said {n} times on each page of a book. How many times do you read it on 2 pages?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count the times on one page, then the same again for the next page.","explain":"{n} on one page and {n} on the next is {=2*n} times. Reading a word that often helps you know it at a glance.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words are in every book. The, said, was, you. If you know them fast, reading feels easy.","Look at said. The s says s. The d says d. But the ai says e, not ai. That bit is the tricky part. Put a heart on it and learn it by heart.","Was and saw have the same letters. But the letters go in a new order, so it is a new word. Look at every letter, from the start.","Not every word we see a lot is tricky. And, it and can sound out. Read them, and the tricky ones, again and again. Soon you know them in a blink."],"adult":["This is word reading in early phonics. The National Curriculum for Year 1 asks children to read common exception words, noting unusual correspondences between spelling and sound and where these occur in the word. Common Core RF.K.3c asks for common high-frequency words read by sight, and RF.1.3g for irregularly spelled words. Phonics programmes often call these tricky words. By 5 to 8, the aim is to read the common ones instantly in connected text.","Reading by sight is not memorising a word's shape. Ehri's work on orthographic mapping shows that a word becomes a sight word when its letters are linked to its sounds and stored. So teach the decodable part through each grapheme-phoneme correspondence (GPC). Then mark the irregular grapheme, the heart part, as the bit to learn by heart. Many high-frequency words, such as and, it and can, are fully decodable. Automaticity comes from many quick, accurate reads, then reading the word in sentences.","Common mistakes: treating every high-frequency word as irregular, so children stop using phonics; guessing from the first letter or the picture, which mixes up was and saw; drilling long lists of flashcards that never appear in real reading; and spelling a tricky word the way it sounds, like sed or thay. Be careful with too many new words at once. Three to five a week, mixed with known ones, works better.","In the lab your child taps the tricky part in said, was, you and the, then catches flashed words and picks was over saw. They build said, was and they from letter tiles that include the sound-alike traps. Then they find the words in real sentences and sort common words into sound it out and tricky part."]},"familyTip":"Write three tricky words on sticky notes and draw a small heart over the tricky part, like the ai in said. Hide them round the room. When your child finds one, they read it, then you both spot it in a bedtime book.","keyIdea":"Some words come up in almost every book: the, said, was, you, they. Good readers know them at a glance, without sounding them out each time. Many of these words still sound out, like and or it. Some have a tricky part that does not say its usual sound, like the ai in said, which says e. Sound out the rest of the word, learn the tricky part by heart, and read the word lots of times until it is quick.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can you sound it out, or does it have a tricky part? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Sound it out","Has a tricky part"],"items":[["it",0],["said",1],["can",0],["you",1],["and",0],["was",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsight","caption":"Flagship lab: tap the tricky part of a word, catch flashed words, build tricky words from tiles, spot them in sentences and sort them.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_PvU3eoikev":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the rime in frog?","choices":["og","rog","g"],"answer":0,"hint":"The rime starts at the vowel. Find the vowel first.","explain":"f and r blend together as the onset, fr. The rime is the vowel and what follows: og."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Blend s + un. What word is it?","choices":["sun","nus","sin"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the onset first, then the rime, quickly: s, un.","explain":"s first, then un, said fast together, makes sun."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The -at family has cat, hat and mat. You add {n} more -at words, like sat and bat. How many -at words is that now?","answer":"3+n","hint":"Start at 3 for cat, hat and mat, then count on.","explain":"3 words and {n} more makes {=3+n} words, all with the same rime, at.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the middle sound in pen?","choices":["e","p","n"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say it slowly: p, e, n. Which sound is between the other two?","explain":"p is first, e is in the middle and n is last. The middle sound is e."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word is in the same word family as hug?","choices":["mug","hat","bag"],"answer":0,"hint":"A word family shares the same rime. What is the rime in hug?","explain":"hug has the rime ug, and so does mug. hat starts like hug, but its rime is at; bag has the rime ag. So only mug is in the -ug family."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A CVC word has 3 sounds. You change 1 sound in each of {n} words to make new words. How many sounds stay the same?","answer":"2*n","hint":"In each word, 1 sound changes and the other 2 stay.","explain":"2 sounds stay in each word, and {n} words, makes {=2*n} sounds that stay the same.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Change the last sound in cat to p. What word is it?","choices":["cap","pat","cot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only the last sound changes. Keep c and a.","explain":"Keep c and a, and change t to p: cap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How do you split chin into onset and rime?","choices":["ch + in","c + hin","chi + n"],"answer":0,"hint":"c and h make one sound here. The rime starts at the vowel i.","explain":"ch is one sound, a digraph, and it comes before the vowel. So chin is ch + in."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Onsets & Rimes: in the word ship, what is the onset?","choices":["sh","s","shi"],"answer":0,"hint":"The onset is every sound before the vowel. Is sh one sound or two?","explain":"s and h together make one sound, sh. It comes before the vowel i, so the onset is sh and the rime is ip."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You make {n} words in the -ug family, like bug and mug. Each one is a CVC word with 3 sounds. How many sounds do you say in all?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each word has a first, a middle and a last sound. Count 3 for every word.","explain":"3 sounds in each word, and {n} words, is {=3*n} sounds. The middle and last sounds are the same each time: u, g.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Change the first sound in dog to l. What new word do you make?","choices":["log","dol","lid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Only the first sound changes. Keep o and g the same.","explain":"Swap d for l and keep og: l + og = log."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Every one-syllable word splits into 2 parts: an onset and a rime. You split {n} words. How many parts is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each word gives you 2 parts. Count 2 for each word.","explain":"2 parts for each of {n} words makes {=2*n} parts: {n} onsets and {n} rimes.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Words have parts. In cat, the first part is c. That is the onset. The rest is at. That is the rime.","Push them together and say it fast: c, at, cat! That is blending. Now swap the onset. h, at, hat. m, at, mat. Same rime, new word.","The rime starts at the vowel. In ship, s and h make one sound, sh. So ship is sh and ip. In frog, f and r go first. So frog is fr and og.","Say dog slowly: d, o, g. d is the first sound, o is the middle, g is the last. Change one sound and you get a new word. Change d to l and dog is log."],"adult":["This is phonics and phonological awareness for ages 4 to 7. In England it sits in the Reception and Year 1 word reading work: children blend phonemes to read words and segment them to spell. In the US, Common Core RF.K.2 asks children to blend and segment onsets and rimes of one-syllable words, isolate the initial, medial vowel and final phonemes in CVC words, and add or substitute phonemes to make new words.","Key terms: a phoneme is one sound; a grapheme is the letter or letters that write it. The onset is the consonant sound or sounds before the vowel; the rime is the vowel and what follows. A digraph such as sh or ch is two letters for one phoneme, so it is one onset sound. A blend such as fr or st is two phonemes said quickly, and both belong to the onset. Words that share a rime, like bug, mug and hug, form a word family.","Common mistakes: putting the vowel in the onset (do + g), splitting a digraph (s + hip), saying the letter name instead of the sound, and adding an uh sound to stop sounds (cuh-at), which makes blending harder. Be careful with the middle sound: children often say the last sound they heard. Ask them to say the word slowly and tap a finger for each sound.","In the lab your child pushes an onset tile onto a rime and picks the matching picture, builds bug, mug and hug from pictures, cuts dog, ship and frog into onset and rime, taps the first, middle or last sound in a CVC word, and swaps one letter to make bat, cap and pen. Vowel tiles are pink with a dot so the start of the rime is easy to see."]},"familyTip":"Play I spy with rimes: 'I spy something that ends in -at… it starts with h.' Then swap: 'Now change the h to c. What is it?' Keep the sounds short: say c, not cuh.","keyIdea":"A one-syllable word splits into an onset and a rime. The onset is the sound or sounds before the vowel, and the rime is the vowel and everything after it: c + at = cat, sh + ip = ship, fr + og = frog. Blending puts them together to say the word, and segmenting splits it apart. In a CVC word each letter is one sound, so you can find the first, middle and last sound, and change just one sound to make a new word, like cat to bat.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which word family? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["The -at family","The -ig family"],"items":[["cat",0],["pig",1],["hat",0],["wig",1],["mat",0],["dig",1],["sat",0],["big",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrime","caption":"Flagship lab: blend onset and rime, build a word family, cut words at the vowel, find first, middle and last sounds, and swap one sound to make a new word.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_F978c32kDr":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The letter c is called see. What sound does c make at the start of cat?","choices":["k","see","sss"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say cat slowly. Is the first sound like see, or like k?","explain":"In cat, c says k. See is the name of the letter, not its sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the best way to say the sound of m?","choices":["mmm","muh","em"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the sound pure. Hum it with your lips shut.","explain":"m says mmm. Muh adds an extra sound, and em is the letter's name."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word has a short a sound, like apple?","choices":["cat","cot","cut"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word slowly and listen to the middle sound.","explain":"Cat has a in the middle, like apple. Cot has o, and cut has u."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In bed, what is the middle sound?","choices":["e, like egg","i, like insect","a, like apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say b-e-d slowly. e and i sound close, so listen carefully.","explain":"Bed has a short e in the middle, like egg. With i it would be bid."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In the word go, what does the o say?","choices":["oh, its name","o, like octopus","g"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say go. Does the o sound like octopus, or like the letter's own name?","explain":"In go, the o says its name, oh. That is a long vowel. In dog, the o is short."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which letter makes the first sound in dog?","choices":["d","b","g"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say dog slowly. b and d look alike, so check which way the letter faces.","explain":"Dog starts with d. b faces the other way and says b, like bus. g is at the end of dog."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You flip {n} letter tiles, and each tile shows one sound. How many sounds do you see?","answer":"n","hint":"One tile shows one sound. Count the tiles.","explain":"Each single letter tile shows one sound, so {n} tiles show {n} sounds.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which word starts with the same sound as fish?","choices":["fan","van","pan"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fish starts with fff. Say each word and listen to its first sound.","explain":"Fan starts with fff, like fish. Van starts with vvv, which is close but buzzes, and pan starts with p."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Sam knows {n} letter sounds. This week he learns the sounds for s, a, t and p. How many letter sounds does Sam know now?","answer":"n+4","hint":"Count the new letters: s, a, t, p. Then start at {n} and count on that many.","explain":"s, a, t and p are 4 new sounds. {n} and 4 more make {=n+4} letter sounds.","params":{"n":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Single letter sounds: the letter s is called ess. What sound does it make at the start of sun?","choices":["sss","ess","suh"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say sun slowly. Listen to the very first sound. Keep it pure, with no uh.","explain":"s says sss, like a snake. Ess is its name, and suh adds an extra uh sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The word cat has 3 letters, and each letter makes one sound. How many sounds are in {n} words like cat?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each word like cat has 3 sounds: k, a, t. Count 3 for each word.","explain":"{n} words with 3 sounds each make {=3*n} sounds. In cat, c says k, a says a, t says t.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which picture word starts with the sound b?","choices":["bus","dog","pig"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each word. Which one starts with a short b sound? Watch out: d faces the other way.","explain":"Bus starts with b. Dog starts with d, and pig starts with p, which is a puff of air."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You know {n} letter sounds. Today you learn 2 more. How many letter sounds do you know now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more.","explain":"{n} and 2 more make {=n+2} letter sounds. A few new sounds each week adds up.","params":{"n":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every letter has a name. This is m. Its name is em. But in a word, m says mmm. Hum it: mmm, like moon.","To read, we use the sound, not the name. c is called see. But in cat it says k. So cat is k, a, t.","Keep each sound short. Say mmm, not muh. Say sss, not suh. Then the sounds join up to make a word.","a, e, i, o and u are vowels. They have short sounds: a like apple, e like egg, i like insect, o like octopus, u like umbrella. A vowel can also say its name. In go, o says oh."],"adult":["This is grapheme-phoneme correspondence, the first step of systematic synthetic phonics. A grapheme is a written letter or group of letters; a phoneme is a single speech sound. Single letter sounds match one letter to its most common phoneme. The EYFS early learning goal asks children to say a sound for each letter of the alphabet, CCSS RF.K.3a asks for the primary sound of each consonant, and RF.K.3b asks for the short and long sounds of the five major vowels. In England, Year 1 children should respond speedily with the correct sound to each grapheme.","Key words: letter name, letter sound (phoneme), grapheme, consonant, vowel, short vowel, long vowel, initial sound, medial sound and CVC word (consonant, vowel, consonant, like cat or bed). A long vowel says its letter name: o in go, e in me, i in hi.","Common mistakes: giving the letter name instead of the sound (see for c), adding an uh to sounds (muh, suh, tuh), which makes blending hard because m-a-t becomes muh-a-tuh; mixing up b and d, which face different ways; and mixing up short e and short i, which sound close in bed and bid. Be careful to stretch continuous sounds (mmm, sss, fff) and keep bouncy ones (b, d, t) short and crisp.","In the lab your child flips letter tiles to see each sound and a picture, drags pictures to the letter that makes their first sound, fills in the missing middle vowel of a CVC word, sorts words by whether the vowel is short or says its name, and plays a quick-sounds round. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Play I spy with sounds, not names: 'I spy something that starts with mmm.' Keep the sound pure. When your child guesses, say the word slowly together, mmm-oo-n, and check the first sound.","keyIdea":"Each letter has a name and a sound. The name of m is em, but the sound it makes in a word is mmm. When you read, you use the sound. Say sounds short and pure, mmm not muh. The five vowels a, e, i, o and u have a short sound, like a in cat, and they can also say their name, like o in go.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which sound does it start with? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["sss, like sun","mmm, like moon","t, like tent"],"items":[["sock",0],["map",1],["top",2],["six",0],["milk",1],["tap",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagphon","caption":"Flagship lab: flip letter tiles, match pictures to first sounds, fill the middle vowel and sort short and long vowels.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__KHQttMde3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Blend the sounds s-u-n. Which word do they make?","choices":["sun","nus","sit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the first sound on the left. Say s, then u, then n, and push them together.","explain":"s-u-n makes sun. Nus reads the sounds backwards, and sit has different sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many sounds are in fish?","choices":["3: f-i-sh","4: f-i-s-h","2: f-sh"],"answer":0,"hint":"s and h team up. Count sounds, not letters.","explain":"Fish has 3 sounds: f, i, sh. The s and h make one sound, and the i must not be skipped."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Tom sees a picture of a pen. Which word should he pick?","choices":["pen","pin","pan"],"answer":0,"hint":"All three words start with p and end with n. Read the middle sound.","explain":"Pen has e in the middle, like egg. Pin has i and pan has a, so read every sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Blend the sounds f-r-o-g. Which word do they make?","choices":["frog","fog","for"],"answer":0,"hint":"f and r sit side by side. Say both of them.","explain":"f-r-o-g makes frog. Fog drops the r, and for has different sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ava says c, a, t and then stops. What should she do next?","choices":["Push the sounds together: cat","Say the letter names","Guess from the picture"],"answer":0,"hint":"Saying the sounds is the first half of reading the word.","explain":"After saying the sounds, she blends them: c-a-t, cat. Letter names and guessing will not read the word."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which two letters make one sound in chop?","choices":["ch","op","co"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a letter team at the start, like sh in ship.","explain":"c and h team up to make ch. So chop has 3 sounds: ch, o, p."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{n} words each have 3 sounds, like map. Each sound gets one sound button. How many sound buttons do you draw in all?","answer":"3*n","hint":"One sound button for each sound. Each word has 3 sounds.","explain":"{n} words with 3 sounds each need {=3*n} sound buttons: one dot for each sound.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"In stop, which two consonants sit side by side at the start?","choices":["s and t","s and o","t and p"],"answer":0,"hint":"Consonants are the letters that are not a, e, i, o or u. Look at the first two letters.","explain":"s and t sit side by side, so you say both: s-t-o-p. o is a vowel, and t and p are not next to each other."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Chick has 5 letters but only 3 sounds, because ch and ck are letter teams. A word has {n} letters and two letter teams. How many sounds does it have?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Each team of two letters makes one sound. Two teams means take 2 away.","explain":"Two letter teams save 2 sounds, so {n} letters make {=n-2} sounds. Chick: ch, i, ck.","params":{"n":[4,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Blend the sounds c-u-p. Which word do they make?","choices":["cup","cap","cut"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say each sound in order, then push them together. Listen to the middle and the last sound.","explain":"c-u-p blends to cup. Cap has a in the middle, and cut ends with t."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. Two of them team up to make one sound, like sh. How many sounds does the word have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Each letter makes one sound, but the team of two makes only one sound. So take 1 away.","explain":"The two team letters share one sound, so {n} letters make {=n-1} sounds. Ship has 4 letters and 3 sounds.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the best way to say the sounds to read map?","choices":["mmm-a-p","muh-a-puh","em-ay-pee"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep each sound short and pure. Do not use the letter names.","explain":"mmm-a-p blends easily into map. Muh-a-puh adds extra uh sounds, and em-ay-pee are the letter names."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Frog has 4 sounds: f-r-o-g. You blend {n} words like frog. How many sounds do you say in all?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Each word like frog has 4 sounds. Count 4 for each word.","explain":"{n} words with 4 sounds each make {=4*n} sounds. Remember to say both f and r in frog.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You can read a word you have never seen. Look at the letters. Say the sound for each one, from left to right. Then push the sounds together. m, a, p. Map!","Keep each sound short. Say mmm, not muh. Say t, not tuh. Extra uh sounds get in the way. Muh-a-puh does not sound like map.","Some letters team up. s and h make one sound: sh. So ship is sh, i, p. It has 4 letters but 3 sounds. c and h make ch, and c and k make one k sound, like in chick.","Look at every letter, not just the first one. Big and bag both start with b. The middle sound tells them apart. In frog, say f and r. Do not skip one."],"adult":["Blending is the core decoding skill in systematic synthetic phonics. The child says the phoneme for each grapheme in order, left to right, then merges the phonemes into a spoken word. The English national curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to respond speedily with the correct sound to graphemes for all 40+ phonemes and to read unfamiliar words by blending the sounds of taught grapheme-phoneme correspondences (GPCs). Common Core RF.K.3 and RF.1.3b ask children to decode regularly spelled one-syllable words, and RF.1.2b asks them to blend sounds orally, including consonant blends.","Key words: phoneme (one speech sound), grapheme (the letter or letters for one sound), digraph (two letters, one sound: sh, ch, th, ck), sound buttons (a dot under a one-letter grapheme and a line under a digraph), CVC word (consonant, vowel, consonant, like map), and adjacent consonants, often called a consonant blend: the f and r in frog make a CCVC word.","Common mistakes: adding uh to each sound (muh-a-puh), which hides the word; saying the sounds but never merging them; reading the two letters of a digraph apart (s-h-i-p); dropping one of two adjacent consonants, so frog is read as fog; and guessing from the first letter or the picture, so bag is read as big. Be careful to hold stretchy sounds (mmm, sss, fff) and clip bouncy ones (p, t, b) short. If blending is hard, practise it out loud first with no print at all.","In the lab your child picks the pure way to say each sound, drags a car under the sound buttons so each sound lights up and then merges into the word, taps letters to join the digraph teams, matches a picture to one of three words that differ by one sound, and blends four-sound words with adjacent consonants. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Play robot talk. Say a word in sounds, like c-u-p, and let your child shout the word. Then swap: point at a cup and let them say it in robot sounds. Keep each sound short, with no uh on the end.","keyIdea":"To read a new word, say the sound for each letter from left to right, then push the sounds together: m-a-p, map. This is called blending. Keep each sound short and pure, mmm not muh. Two letters can make one sound, like sh in ship, so ship has 4 letters but 3 sounds. When two consonants sit side by side, as in frog, say both. Read every sound, not just the first, so you can tell bag from big.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Count the sounds, not the letters. Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["3 sounds","4 sounds"],"items":[["ship",0],["frog",1],["chick",0],["stop",1],["fish",0],["crab",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagblend","caption":"Flagship lab: say pure sounds, drive the blending car, join digraph teams, read every sound and blend longer words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ZhvwM6LMBL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Leo reads very, very fast. He runs past every full stop. What goes wrong?","choices":["It stops making sense","Nothing, fast is best","His voice goes up too much"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what the full stops are for.","explain":"Racing past the full stops runs the ideas together, so it stops making sense. Fluent reading is talking speed, not a race."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which book is just right to read by yourself?","choices":["I can read 9 of 10 words","I can read 6 of 10 words","I can read 4 of 10 words"],"answer":0,"hint":"Just right means only a few words need sounding out.","explain":"9 of 10 words is just right. With 6 or 4 of 10 there are too many stops, so read those books with a grown-up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"We won the race! How do you read this line?","choices":["With a strong, happy voice","In a quiet, flat voice","Like asking a question"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the mark at the end of the line.","explain":"An exclamation mark asks for a strong voice that shows the feeling. A question would end with a question mark."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the best way to get smoother at reading a book?","choices":["Read it again and again","Read it once and stop","Look at the pictures and guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what happens to the stops each time you read the same page.","explain":"Rereading the same book makes the words quicker each time, so the reading gets smoother. Guessing from pictures skips the real words."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Where does the first scoop end in: The little bird sang in the tree.","choices":["After bird","After The","After sang in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: who? Then ask: what did it do?","explain":"Who? The little bird. What did it do? Sang in the tree. So the first scoop ends after bird."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Zoe reads: The… dog… is… wet. What can she try?","choices":["Read it in scoops: The dog… is wet.","Read it faster with no stops","Say each word louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"One word at a time sounds like a robot. Group the words.","explain":"Reading in scoops, The dog… is wet, sounds like talking. Going faster or louder does not group the words."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A line has {n} scoops. Each scoop has 3 words. How many words are in the line?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Count 3 words for each scoop.","explain":"{n} scoops of 3 words make {=3*n} words. You read each scoop in one go.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam reads a page of 10 words and can read {n} of them. How many does he get stuck on?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Take the words he can read away from 10.","explain":"10 take away {n} is {=10-n}. If he gets stuck on more than 1 word in 10, read the book together.","params":{"n":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Is the cat asleep? and The cat is asleep. have the same words. How do you read them?","choices":["Differently: the question goes up","The same, because the words match","Both loud and strong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the two end marks.","explain":"The end mark changes the voice. The question goes up, and the full stop line is calm."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which way of reading sounds most like talking?","choices":["My cat… sat on the mat.","My… cat… sat… on… the… mat.","Mycatsatonthemat."],"answer":0,"hint":"Talking puts words that go together into one group, a scoop.","explain":"My cat… sat on the mat reads in two scoops, like talking. One word at a time sounds like a robot, and running it all together is too fast to follow."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A page has 10 words. You get stuck on {n} of them. How many words can you read?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Take the words you got stuck on away from 10.","explain":"10 take away {n} is {=10-n}. If that is 9 or 10, the book is just right for reading by yourself.","params":{"n":[0,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A line ends with a question mark: Where is my hat? How should your voice go?","choices":["Up at the end, like asking","Flat, the same as every line","Loud and strong, like shouting"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the mark at the end of the line. What does it tell you?","explain":"A question mark means you are asking, so your voice goes up. A strong voice is for an exclamation mark, and a flat voice hides the question."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia reads a page and gets stuck {n} times. Each time she reads it again, she gets stuck 1 time fewer. How many times does she get stuck on her next read?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Take 1 away from the number of times she got stuck.","explain":"{n} take away 1 is {=n-1}. Each reread makes the words easier, so the stops go down.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Fluent reading sounds like talking. A robot reads like this: the… big… dog… ran. You can read in scoops. The big dog… ran to the park. Each scoop is a group of words that go together.","Look at the mark at the end. A full stop says: stop, and be calm. A question mark says: you are asking, so let your voice go up. An exclamation mark says: use a strong voice!","Fast is not the best. Too fast and you run past the stops, and it does not make sense. Too slow and you forget the start. Read at talking speed.","Read the same book again. The first time, you may get stuck and sound out a word. The next time it is quicker. Pick books where you can read 9 or 10 words out of 10."],"adult":["Fluency means reading the words correctly, at a natural pace, with expression. Experts call the expression part prosody. When reading is fluent, your child has more attention left for meaning. The English national curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to read aloud books that match the phonics they know, and to reread them to build fluency and confidence. In Year 2 they read such books accurately, automatically and without undue hesitation. Common Core RF.K.4 asks for emergent-reader texts read with purpose and understanding. RF.1.4b asks for reading aloud with accuracy, appropriate rate and expression on successive readings.","Key words: accuracy (words read correctly), automaticity (knowing a word at once, with no slow sounding out), rate (how many words are read correctly in a minute), prosody (phrasing, stress and the rise and fall of the voice), phrasing (reading in groups of words that belong together, often called scooping), repeated reading (reading the same text a few times) and a decodable book (one that uses only the phonics your child has been taught). This topic asks for reading with at least 90% accuracy, about 9 words in 10. Many teachers aim higher, about 19 words in 20, for reading alone. Below 9 in 10, read the book together. The DfE reading framework adds that a decodable book should match the sounds already taught, so every word can be sounded out.","Common mistakes: treating speed as the goal, so children race past full stops and lose the meaning; robot reading, one word at a time; a flat voice that ignores question and exclamation marks; books that are too hard, so every line stops for sounding out; reading a book once and moving on; and guessing words from the picture. Be careful not to time children with a stopwatch at this age. Praise smooth rereading with a voice that fits.","In the lab your child taps where the first scoop of a line ends. They drag a calm, asking or excited voice onto lines that end in a full stop, question mark or exclamation mark. They read one page three times and see the stops fall from 3 to 1 to none. They sort four books by how many words in 10 they can read. Last, they drag a speed dial to hear why snail and rocket reading both lose the meaning. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Pick a short book your child knows well. Read one page to them like a storyteller, then let them read the same page back to you. Read it again tomorrow. Point out one thing that got better, like a smooth scoop or a question that went up.","keyIdea":"Fluent reading sounds like talking. You read the words right, at talking speed, with a voice that fits. Read in scoops, groups of words that go together, not one word at a time. Let the end mark set your voice: calm for a full stop, up for a question mark, strong for an exclamation mark. Pick a book where you can read 9 or 10 words out of 10, and read it again and again. Each time, the stops get fewer and the reading gets smoother.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fluent or not yet? Drag each way of reading, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fluent","Not yet"],"items":[["Reads in scoops, like talking",0],["Reads one… word… at… a… time",1],["Voice goes up at a question mark",0],["Races past every full stop",1],["Reads the book again to get smoother",0],["Gets stuck on 5 words in 10",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfluent","caption":"Flagship lab: scoop words into phrases, set the voice from the end mark, reread a page, pick a just right book and find talking speed.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9-OHslmt1g":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many sounds are in chip?","choices":["3: ch-i-p","4: c-h-i-p","2: ch-p"],"answer":0,"hint":"c and h are a team. Count sounds, not letters.","explain":"Chip has 3 sounds: ch, i, p. The c and h make one sound, and the i must not be skipped."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which word is spelled right?","choices":["duck","duk","duc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Listen to the vowel just before the k sound. Is it short?","explain":"Duck has a short u just before the k sound, so it ends in ck."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which word starts with sh?","choices":["sheep","sun","cheese"],"answer":0,"hint":"sh sounds like a quiet finger on your lips: shh.","explain":"Sheep starts with sh. Sun starts with s on its own, and cheese starts with ch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What sound does wh make in wheel?","choices":["w","h","wuh-huh"],"answer":0,"hint":"w and h are a team. Say wheel out loud.","explain":"wh says w, so wheel starts with a w sound. The two letters make one sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A little stick that lights a fire. Which spelling is right?","choices":["match","mach","matsh"],"answer":0,"hint":"The a in match is short. What comes after a short vowel?","explain":"After a short vowel, the ch sound is spelled tch: match, catch, fetch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word ends with ng?","choices":["ring","rig","rich"],"answer":0,"hint":"ng is the sound at the end of sing.","explain":"Ring ends with ng, one sound. Rig ends with g, and rich ends with ch."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Chick has 5 letters but only 3 sounds, because ch and ck are teams. A word has {n} letters and two teams. How many sounds does it have?","answer":"n-2","hint":"Each team of two letters makes one sound. Two teams means take 2 away.","explain":"Two teams save 2 sounds, so {n} letters make {=n-2} sounds. Chick: ch, i, ck.","params":{"n":[4,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Mia draws one line under each team of two letters. She draws {n} lines for words like sh, ch and th. How many letters are in her teams?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each team has 2 letters. Count 2 for each line.","explain":"{n} teams of 2 letters make {=2*n} letters, but only {n} sounds.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Which word ends in just k, not ck?","choices":["milk","duck","sock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the letter just before the k sound. Is it a vowel or a consonant?","explain":"In milk, l comes just before the k sound, so it is just k. Duck and sock have a short vowel before, so they use ck."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which two letters make one sound in fish?","choices":["sh","fi","is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say fish slowly. Listen for the sound at the end.","explain":"s and h team up to say sh. Fish has 3 sounds: f, i, sh."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A word has {n} letters. One pair of letters is a team, like ck in duck. How many sounds does the word have?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Each letter makes a sound, but the team of two makes only one. Take 1 away.","explain":"The two team letters share one sound, so {n} letters make {=n-1} sounds. Duck has 4 letters and 3 sounds.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What sound does ph make in dolphin?","choices":["f","p","h"],"answer":0,"hint":"p and h are a team. Say dolphin out loud.","explain":"ph says f, so dolphin sounds like dol-fin. It does not say p."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ship has 3 sounds: sh-i-p. You read {n} words like ship. How many sounds do you say in all?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each word like ship has 3 sounds. Count 3 for each word.","explain":"{n} words with 3 sounds each make {=3*n} sounds. The sh counts as one sound each time.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some letters work as a team. s and h stick together and say sh. Ship is sh, i, p. One team, one sound.","Teams to know: sh in ship, ch in chip, th in bath, ng in ring and ck in duck. Ship and chip look the same at the end. The team at the start makes them two words.","Two teams play a trick. p and h say f, like in photo. w and h say w, like in wheel. Count sounds, not letters. Duck has 4 letters. Say it slowly: d, u, ck. That is 3 sounds.","Duck ends in ck, and match ends in tch. That is because a short vowel sound comes just before. Milk ends in k, as l comes just before."],"adult":["A consonant digraph is two letters that spell one consonant phoneme. Year 1 of the English national curriculum teaches ch, sh, th, ng and ck, the /k/ sound spelt ck after a single short vowel, tch for /tʃ/ after a single short vowel (with exceptions such as rich, which, much and such), ph and wh in words like dolphin and wheel, and k rather than c before e, i and y (kit, skip). Common Core RF.1.3a asks children to know the spelling-sound links for common consonant digraphs.","Key words: phoneme (one sound), grapheme (the letter or letters that spell it), digraph (two letters, one sound), trigraph (three letters, one sound, such as tch), and sound buttons: a dot under a single letter and a line under a digraph. Children who segment by sound can spell; those who count letters cannot.","Common mistakes: reading the two letters apart (s-h-i-p), which breaks the word; counting letters instead of sounds; reading ph as p; hearing sh in any word that starts with s, such as snake; and writing duk for duck or mach for match. Be careful with th: it has a quiet sound in thin and a buzzy sound in that, but it is the same digraph.","In the lab your child drags sh, ch or th into a word and lets the picture decide (ship or chip), counts sounds in duck, ring and chick, sorts ph and wh words by the sound they make, chooses ck or k and tch or ch to finish a word, and hunts the pictures that start with sh. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Go on a sound hunt at home. Find three things that start with sh, ch or th: a shoe, a chair, a thumb. Say each one slowly and ask: is it one sound or two at the start?","keyIdea":"A consonant digraph is two letters that make one sound: sh in ship, ch in chip, th in bath, ng in ring and ck in duck. Count sounds, not letters, so duck has 4 letters but 3 sounds. ph says f, as in photo, and wh says w, as in wheel. At the end of a short word, straight after a short vowel, the k sound is spelled ck (duck) and the ch sound is spelled tch (match). After a consonant it is just k or ch: milk, lunch.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which team starts the word? Drag each word, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["sh","ch","th"],"items":[["shop",0],["chin",1],["thin",2],["shed",0],["chop",1],["that",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdgraph","caption":"Flagship lab: drag digraph magnets into words, count sounds not letters, sort ph and wh, pick ck or tch endings and hunt for sh.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_t1JXeNgKcu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does can't mean?","choices":["cannot","can it","can too"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word has the letters c, a, n, n, o, t?","explain":"Can't is short for cannot. The n and o drop out and the apostrophe goes in their place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does it's mean in: It's raining?","choices":["it is","it was","its"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try saying it is in its place. Does the sentence still make sense?","explain":"It's is it is: the i of is drops out. It is raining makes sense."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does the apostrophe in we'll show?","choices":["Letters are missing","The word has ended","Someone owns it"],"answer":0,"hint":"We'll is short for two words. What happened to some letters?","explain":"We'll is we will. The w and i drop out and the apostrophe shows where."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the short way to say they are?","choices":["they're","theyare","the'yre"],"answer":0,"hint":"The a of are drops out. Put the apostrophe where it was.","explain":"They are becomes they're. The apostrophe takes the place of the a."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which word is a contraction? We're going home.","choices":["We're","going","home"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the word with the little mark in it.","explain":"We're has an apostrophe. It is short for we are."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A story has {a} contractions on page 1 and {b} on page 2. How many contractions are in the story?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the contractions on both pages.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} contractions. Each one hides two words.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam writes I'm {n} times. Each time the a of am drops out. How many letters did he leave out in all?","answer":"n","hint":"One letter goes each time. How many times did he write I'm?","explain":"One a drops out each time, so {n} times leaves out {n} letters.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is right: I'm or I'am?","choices":["I'm","I'am","Both are right"],"answer":0,"hint":"The apostrophe takes the place of a letter. Should the letter stay too?","explain":"I'm is right. The apostrophe replaces the a, so the a does not stay."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does I'm mean?","choices":["I am","I will","I can"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the two long words. Which letter dropped out of I am?","explain":"I'm is I am squashed together. The apostrophe sits where the a of am was."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Tom reads {a} words with an apostrophe for missing letters, like don't. He also reads {b} words with no apostrophe. How many contractions does he read?","answer":"a","hint":"A contraction has an apostrophe for missing letters. Count only those words.","explain":"Only the {a} words with an apostrophe are contractions. The {b} other words are not.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one has the apostrophe in the right place?","choices":["don't","do'nt","dont'"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not loses the o from not. Where was that o?","explain":"Don't puts the apostrophe where the o of not was, between the n and the t."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Kim writes {n} sentences. In each one she squashes do not into don't. How many apostrophes does she write?","answer":"n","hint":"Each don't has one apostrophe. How many don'ts are there?","explain":"Each don't needs one apostrophe, so {n} sentences need {n} apostrophes.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Sometimes we squash two words into one. I am turns into I'm. It is quicker to say.","When the words squash, some letters drop out. A little mark goes in the gap. It is called an apostrophe.","The apostrophe sits right where the letters went. In don't, it sits where the o of not was.","When you read a short word like can't, think of the long words. Can't means cannot. It's means it is."],"adult":["In England, Year 1 word reading asks children to read words with contractions, such as I'm, I'll and we'll. They should know that the apostrophe stands for the letter or letters left out. In Year 2 they learn to spell these words with the apostrophe in the right place, so reading them well now helps with writing next year.","The words to use: a contraction is a short form of two words. The apostrophe of omission marks where letters have been left out. Not is the most common partner (don't, can't, didn't, isn't), along with am, is, are, will and have (I'm, she's, they're, we'll, I've). Can't comes from cannot, which is already one word, so only the n and o drop.","Common mistakes: putting the apostrophe between the two words (do'nt, not don't); leaving it out (Im, dont); keeping the dropped letter too (I'am); and mixing up we'll with well, or it's with its. Be careful not to teach it's and its with rules about owning yet. At this age the simple test is: can you say it is in its place?","In the lab your child taps out the letters that drop and drags the apostrophe into the gap. Then they unzip short words to their long forms, pick the right gap in dont, shes and theyre, spot three contractions in a tiny story, and squash two words in a sentence into one. Every drag also has a big button."]},"familyTip":"When you read together, stop at a word like don't or I'm. Ask: which two words are hiding in here? Cover the apostrophe with a finger and ask which letters went missing.","keyIdea":"A contraction is two words squashed into one short word. Some letters drop out, and an apostrophe ( ' ) goes in their place. I am becomes I'm, do not becomes don't, and we will becomes we'll. To read a contraction, say the two long words in your head: can't means cannot, it's means it is.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Contraction or not? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Contraction","No apostrophe"],"items":[["don't",0],["dog",1],["I'm",0],["jam",1],["we'll",0],["well",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcontr","caption":"Flagship lab: squash two words into one, unzip short words, find the apostrophe's gap, spot contractions in a story.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
